the-firm-as-one-program-lines-31 : List (List Word)
the-firm-as-one-program-lines-31 = cons "- **S1** The gains multiply instead of adding, which changes the firm's rate of learning (all of layer 3)." (cons "  The lines do not add; they multiply. Faster iteration means more strategies tested, cumulative knowledge means each test builds on all earlier ones, proven risk means more capital behind each, and exact reasoning under uncertainty means better decisions per unit of capital. Artificial researchers multiply all of it again. The result is a firm whose rate of learning is itself rising. Its advantage over competitors is a difference in kind, not a margin." (cons "- **S2** The market concentrates toward the firm that has this ([S1])." (cons "  Electronic trading is close to a zero-sum contest between firms over the same flow, and market share already concentrates toward the firms with the best technology. A discontinuity in technology produces a discontinuity in concentration: the firm that has this takes opportunities first, correctly, at full size, everywhere, and its competitors trade against a counterparty that is better informed and quicker to adapt. That is why a figure of several billion understates it." (cons "- **S3** Prices converge on what is true, so the edge that profits came from shrinks ([S2])." (cons "  Edge in markets is information not yet reflected in prices. As the firm extracts it, prices become correct sooner, and the edge available to everyone shrinks. The firm profits precisely by removing the ignorance that made profit possible. Profit in trading measures collective ignorance, and this system dispels collective ignorance at machine speed." (cons "- **S4** What Hayek argued prices must carry becomes directly available, so prices stop being the main way the economy coordinates ([S3], [P9])." (cons "  Hayek argued in 1945 that no central planner could hold the knowledge dispersed across a society, so prices had to carry it in compressed form. Hyperactivity is not a central planner: it has no canonical state, every participant holds its own, and constructions are exchanged between peers. But the knowledge that prices compress becomes available directly, with its evidence, to every participant. Once that holds, prices stop being the primary carrier of coordination, and an economy built around competing partial knowledge gives way to one built around shared knowledge. The trading firm is where this starts, because no institution has more money riding on the gap between what prices know and what is true. The same mechanism, aimed at knowledge instead of money, is the dispelling of ignorance across the human superorganism: thousands of minds in one firm becoming one reasoning process is the small case of billions of minds doing the same." (cons "" nil))))))))
